      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 5,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\nFind the moment of inertia of this plate about the line $x=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, the distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-2|.\n$$\n\nNext, the area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=3\\cdot 1=3.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{M}{3}|x-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{M}{3}(x-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}({\\int }_{2}^{5}(x-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}\\cdot [\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)^{3}]_{2}^{5}\\cdot {[y]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}(5-2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}(2-2)^{3})\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}(9-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3M.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
